Sure! Here's a full example of using the `@Cacheable` annotation in a Spring Boot application:

First, let's assume that you have a service class called `BookService` that retrieves book information from a database. You want to cache the results of the method `getBookDetails()` to improve performance. Here's how you can do it:

1. Add the necessary dependencies to your `pom.xml` file:

```xml
<dependencies>
   <dependency>
      <groupId>org.springframework.boot</groupId>
      <artifactId>spring-boot-starter-cache</artifactId>
   </dependency>
   <!-- Other dependencies -->
</dependencies>
```

2. Configure caching in your Spring Boot application. You can do this by creating a configuration class, let's call it `CachingConfig`:

```java
import org.springframework.cache.annotation.EnableCaching;
import org.springframework.context.annotation.Configuration;

@Configuration
@EnableCaching
public class CachingConfig {
   // Additional configuration if needed
}
```

3. In your `BookService` class, annotate the `getBookDetails()` method with `@Cacheable`:

```java
import org.springframework.cache.annotation.CacheConfig;
import org.springframework.cache.annotation.Cacheable;
import org.springframework.stereotype.Service;

@Service
@CacheConfig(cacheNames = "books")
public class BookService {

   @Cacheable
   public Book getBookDetails(String bookId) {
      // Logic to fetch book details from database
      // ...
      return book;
   }
}
```

4. Finally, you can use the `BookService` in your controller or any other class:

```java
import org.springframework.beans.factory.annotation.Autowired;
import org.springframework.web.bind.annotation.GetMapping;
import org.springframework.web.bind.annotation.PathVariable;
import org.springframework.web.bind.annotation.RestController;

@RestController
public class BookController {

   @Autowired
   private BookService bookService;

   @GetMapping("/books/{id}")
   public Book getBookDetails(@PathVariable("id") String bookId) {
      return bookService.getBookDetails(bookId);
   }
}
```

Now, when the `getBookDetails()` method is called with a specific `bookId`, Spring will check if the result is already present in the cache. If it is, the cached result will be returned directly without executing the method again. If it is not, the method will be executed, and the result will be stored in the cache for future use.

Remember to configure a cache implementation, such as Redis or Ehcache, for this to work properly. Additionally, you can customize caching further by specifying the cache name, cache key, eviction policies, and more.

To make the cache entries expire after 5 minutes, you need to configure the cache with an appropriate TTL (Time to Live) or expiration policy. Here's how you can do it using Spring Boot and the `@Cacheable` annotation:

1. First, make sure you have a cache implementation (such as Redis or Ehcache) configured and added as a dependency in your project.

2. In your `CachingConfig` class, specify the cache duration using the `defaultExpiration` attribute. This sets a global expiration time for all cache entries:

```java
import org.springframework.cache.annotation.EnableCaching;
import org.springframework.context.annotation.Configuration;
import org.springframework.cache.annotation.CacheConfig;
import org.springframework.cache.annotation.CachingConfigurerSupport;
import org.springframework.cache.concurrent.ConcurrentMapCacheManager;
import java.util.concurrent.TimeUnit;

@Configuration
@EnableCaching
public class CachingConfig extends CachingConfigurerSupport {

   @Bean
   @Override
   public CacheManager cacheManager() {
       return new ConcurrentMapCacheManager("books");
   }

   @Bean
   public CacheManager cacheManager() {
       SimpleCacheManager cacheManager = new SimpleCacheManager();
       cacheManager.setCaches(Arrays.asList(new ConcurrentMapCache("books")));
       cacheManager.setDefaultExpiration(TimeUnit.MINUTES.toSeconds(5));
       return cacheManager;
   }
}
```

3. Next, annotate the `getBookDetails()` method in your `BookService` class with `@Cacheable` and specify an additional property `@Cacheable(value = "books", key = "#bookId", unless = "#result == null")`. This ensures that each cache entry has an individual expiration time:

```java
@Service
@CacheConfig(cacheNames = "books")
public class BookService {

   @Cacheable(value = "books", key = "#bookId", unless = "#result == null")
   public Book getBookDetails(String bookId) {
      // Logic to fetch book details from database
      // ...
      return book;
   }
}
```

4. Now, every time the `getBookDetails()` method is called, its result will be cached with an expiration time of 5 minutes. After 5 minutes, the cache entry will be automatically evicted, and subsequent calls to the method with the same `bookId` value will trigger a new execution and caching.

Make sure to adjust the cache configuration according to your chosen cache implementation. This example uses `ConcurrentMapCacheManager` as a simple in-memory cache manager.

To connect to a local database and run SQL commands using .NET Framework 4.8, you can use the classes provided by the `System.Data.SqlClient` namespace for SQL Server databases, or the corresponding namespace for other databases (like `MySql.Data.MySqlClient` for MySQL, `Npgsql` for PostgreSQL, etc).

Here is an example using SQL Server:

First, ensure that you have added the `System.Data.SqlClient` reference to your project.

Then, in your C# code, you would do something like this:

```csharp
using System;
using System.Data.SqlClient;

class Program
{
    static void Main(string[] args)
    {
        // Connection string for your local database
        // Make sure to modify the DataSource and InitialCatalog according to your setup
        // Integrated Security is set to true for Windows Authentication
        // For SQL Server Authentication, you will need to specify UserID and Password
        string connectionString = "Data Source=.;Initial Catalog=YourDatabaseName;Integrated Security=True";

        // SQL command you want to execute
        string sql = "SELECT * FROM YourTableName";

        // Create a SqlConnection using the provided connection string
        using (SqlConnection connection = new SqlConnection(connectionString))
        {
            try
            {
                // Open the SqlConnection
                connection.Open();
                Console.WriteLine("Connection opened successfully.");

                // Create a SqlCommand using the connection and the SQL string
                using (SqlCommand command = new SqlCommand(sql, connection))
                {
                    // Execute the SqlCommand and obtain a SqlDataReader
                    using (SqlDataReader reader = command.ExecuteReader())
                    {
                        // Read the data
                        while (reader.Read())
                        {
                            // Assuming your table has at least one column named 'ColumnName'
                            Console.WriteLine(reader["ColumnName"].ToString());
                        }
                    }
                }
            }
            catch (Exception ex)
            {
                // Handle any exceptions that occur
                Console.WriteLine("An error occurred: " + ex.Message);
            }
            finally
            {
                // Close the connection if it's open
                if (connection.State == System.Data.ConnectionState.Open)
                    connection.Close();
                Console.WriteLine("Connection closed.");
            }
        }

        Console.WriteLine("Press any key to exit...");
        Console.ReadKey();
    }
}
```

Before running the code, make sure the database server is running, the database exists, and you've correctly replaced `YourDatabaseName`, `YourTableName`, and the `DataSource` in the connection string with the relevant details of your local setup.

Additionally, if you want to execute a non-query command (e.g., `INSERT`, `UPDATE`, `DELETE`), you would use the `ExecuteNonQuery` method of the `SqlCommand` object:

```csharp
string sql = "INSERT INTO YourTableName (Column1, Column2) VALUES ('Value1', 'Value2')";
using (SqlCommand command = new SqlCommand(sql, connection))
{
    int affectedRows = command.ExecuteNonQuery();
    Console.WriteLine($"Number of rows affected: {affectedRows}");
}
```

For parameterized queries (to prevent SQL injection), use parameters like so:

```csharp
